# `nmeshctl` — the node from a terminal

For an operator who reached the machine over SSH. `nmeshctl` talks to the node's
own console on this machine, and through it to every node that granted it
`manage` — the same session, the same operations, the same refusals as the web
console. It is not a second implementation of anything: the commands are read
from the node's control-plane catalogue
([`../Architecture/control-plane.md`](../Architecture/control-plane.md)), so an
operation the node gains is a command the moment it does.

Source: [`scripts/nmeshctl.py`](../../scripts/nmeshctl.py). Standard library
only, and no import of the node's code: a management tool that needed liboqs to
start would not start on the day it is needed most.

## Installing

`install.sh` copies it to `/usr/local/bin/nmeshctl` (a root install),
`~/.local/bin/nmeshctl` (a user install) or `$PREFIX/bin/nmeshctl` (Termux). The
file itself, not a wrapper: the install directory is mode 700 to the node's
own account, so an administrator signed in over SSH could not read a script
left there. `--uninstall` removes it.

A node that updates itself replaces its own tree, not this copy — re-run
`install.sh` (or copy `scripts/nmeshctl.py`) to bring the command line up to the
node's version. Nothing breaks in between: the commands come from the node.

## Signing in

```bash
nmeshctl login                      # 8 hours
nmeshctl login --for 1d             # at most 24 h
nmeshctl login --url https://127.0.0.1:8787 --label laptop
nmeshctl status                     # until when, on which node
nmeshctl logout
```

The password is asked for without echo (or read from `--password-stdin`, or
`NMESH_PASSWORD` for a script). What comes back is a session that **survives
the node's restarts** — every update is one — and ends on the date it was
given, whether or not it is used. It is kept in `~/.config/nmesh/ctl.json`
(0600; `NMESH_CTL_CONFIG` to put it elsewhere), and on the node only as a hash.
It ends at `logout`, when the console password is changed, and at
`install.sh --reset-password`. Details:
[`../Architecture/security.md`](../Architecture/security.md#a-session-that-outlives-the-process-console_sessionspy).

**The console's certificate is pinned at login.** It is self-signed, so trust
comes from its fingerprint, compared before a byte of any request is sent:

- read from the node's state directory (`/var/lib/nmesh`, or `NMESH_DATA`) when
  this account can — which needs no question;
- otherwise shown once for you to confirm, as SSH does — compare it with the
  one `install.sh` prints, or pass `--fingerprint <sha256>`; `--yes` pins
  without asking, and says so.

A certificate that changes afterwards is never spoken to: sign in again if it
was regenerated on purpose.

## Operations

```bash
nmeshctl ops                         # everything this console can reach
nmeshctl ops config                  # one module
nmeshctl node state
nmeshctl node ping --node <id>       # after the command, --node is the operation's own
nmeshctl config save --settings '{"pseudo": "box"}'
nmeshctl trace set --action start --seconds 60
nmeshctl logs query --level warn --limit 50
nmeshctl call node.state --params '{}'   # the raw door, JSON arguments
```

`<module> <operation>` and then the arguments the operation declares, as
`--name value`, `--name=value` or `name=value`; a bare `--flag` is yes. A
structure (`document`, `payload`) is JSON. An argument the operation does not
take is refused here with the list of those it does — never passed on.

What the answer is: JSON on standard output. `--json` anywhere on the line
does the same for the commands that print tables. Exit codes: `0` done, `1`
refused or failed, `2` the command line was wrong, `3` signed out.

**Long operations wait.** A node driven from a distance answers anything
longer than one relayed call with "start it as a job"; `nmeshctl` does exactly
that, says so on standard error, and polls until the answer comes. Locally the
same operation simply takes as long as it takes.

## Another node

```bash
nmeshctl remote list                         # the nodes that granted this one manage
nmeshctl --node web-01 node state            # by id, id prefix, label or name
nmeshctl --node web-01 config save --settings '{"pseudo": "web"}'
nmeshctl remote connect web-01               # open the session explicitly
nmeshctl remote disconnect web-01
nmeshctl --node web-01 --then <id> node state    # one further, through web-01
```

`--node` (before the command) opens this console's session on that node the way
the web console's context selector does: nothing to type where the node granted
`passwordless`, that node's console password otherwise — asked for, sent once
inside the mesh session, kept nowhere. Every command then runs there, refused or
answered by that node from its own ledger.

`--then` reaches a node *that one* manages, through it. It needs that node's
**`full`** grant — the one that makes it a jump host — and is refused otherwise.

## Rights: who may manage what

The fleet app's ledger, from here. Run on the node that bears the consequence
(or `--node` one that granted this one `full`).

```bash
nmeshctl fleet caps                          # the capabilities and what each allows
nmeshctl fleet requests                      # nodes asking to manage this one
nmeshctl fleet approve laptop                # grant what it asked for
nmeshctl fleet approve laptop status,manage  # …or less of it — never more
nmeshctl fleet deny laptop --reason "not this one"
nmeshctl fleet operators                     # who controls this node, with what
nmeshctl fleet grant laptop manage apps      # set exactly this
nmeshctl fleet grant laptop shell --add      # add to what it holds
nmeshctl fleet grant laptop shell --remove   # take away
nmeshctl fleet grant laptop full             # everything (see below)
nmeshctl fleet revoke laptop                 # cut it off, both ways
nmeshctl fleet managed                       # the nodes this one manages
nmeshctl fleet request web-01 --caps manage,apps   # ask another node for rights
nmeshctl fleet drop web-01 shell             # hand one back
```

The rules are the fleet's own ([`../Apps/fleet`](../Apps/fleet)): an approval
can only **narrow** what was asked, and `nmeshctl` refuses a wider one before
sending it; a capability name it does not know is refused rather than dropped,
because a typo must not become "no rights"; asking for more parks a request for
a human on the other node.

Two grants matter most from a terminal:

| Grant | What it adds |
|---|---|
| `apps` | the node's apps from a remote console: chat, and every operation an app declares |
| `full` | everything: every capability, every console operation as if at the machine, and the nodes it manages through it |

## Bounds

| Bound | Value | Why |
|---|---|---|
| session lifetime | 1 min – 24 h, default 8 h | a credential in a file ends on a date |
| lasting sessions per node | 16, oldest first | a login loop cannot fill the disk |
| an answer read | 4 MB | the console's own replies are far smaller; a larger one is refused, not truncated |
| waiting on a job | 15 min | then it says how to look at it again (`nmeshctl jobs poll --job …`) |
